WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.136 --> 00:04.026 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference November 5,

00:04.026 --> 00:05.246 align:middle
2020 CHAIR POWELL.

00:05.246 --> 00:05.576 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:05.766 --> 00:10.346 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:10.346 --> 00:12.356 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:12.356 --> 00:15.086 align:middle
that Congress has given
us-maximum employment

00:15.086 --> 00:15.966 align:middle
and price stability.

00:16.996 --> 00:18.456 align:middle
Since the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:18.686 --> 00:21.936 align:middle
we have taken forceful actions
to provide relief and stability,

00:22.486 --> 00:25.236 align:middle
to ensure that the recovery
will be as strong as possible,

00:25.746 --> 00:27.956 align:middle
and to limit lasting
damage to the economy.

00:29.236 --> 00:32.046 align:middle
Today my colleagues on the
Federal Open Market Committee

00:32.046 --> 00:35.096 align:middle
and I reaffirmed our commitment
to support the economy

00:35.096 --> 00:36.356 align:middle
in this challenging time.

00:38.176 --> 00:40.596 align:middle
Economic activity has
continued to recover

00:40.596 --> 00:42.686 align:middle
from its depressed
second-quarter level.

00:43.816 --> 00:47.436 align:middle
The reopening of the economy led
to a rapid rebound in activity,

00:48.356 --> 00:50.586 align:middle
and real GDP rose
at an annual rate

00:50.586 --> 00:52.686 align:middle
of 33 percent in
the third quarter.

00:54.396 --> 00:55.726 align:middle
In recent months, however,

00:55.726 --> 00:57.546 align:middle
the pace of improvement
has moderated.

00:58.846 --> 01:01.866 align:middle
Household spending on goods,
especially durable goods,

01:02.286 --> 01:05.626 align:middle
has been strong and has moved
above its pre-pandemic level.

01:06.116 --> 01:10.896 align:middle
In contrast, spending
on services remains low,

01:11.506 --> 01:14.086 align:middle
largely due to ongoing
weakness in sectors

01:14.086 --> 01:16.466 align:middle
that typically require
people to gather closely,

01:16.656 --> 01:18.896 align:middle
including travel
and hospitality.

01:20.306 --> 01:23.066 align:middle
The overall rebound in household
spending owes, in part,

01:23.066 --> 01:26.406 align:middle
to federal stimulus payments and
expanded unemployment benefits,

01:26.796 --> 01:28.406 align:middle
which provided essential support

01:28.406 --> 01:30.286 align:middle
to many families
and individuals.

01:30.796 --> 01:34.786 align:middle
The housing sector has fully
recovered from the downturn,

01:35.046 --> 01:37.716 align:middle
supported in part by low
mortgage interest rates.

01:37.766 --> 01:42.496 align:middle
Business investment
has also picked up.

01:42.746 --> 01:46.516 align:middle
Even so, overall economic
activity remains well below its

01:46.516 --> 01:47.786 align:middle
level before the pandemic,

01:47.986 --> 01:50.126 align:middle
and the path ahead
remains highly uncertain.

01:50.566 --> 01:55.186 align:middle
In the labor market, roughly
half of the 22 million jobs

01:55.186 --> 01:58.006 align:middle
that were lost in March and
April have been regained,

01:58.136 --> 02:00.286 align:middle
as many people were
able to return to work.

02:01.746 --> 02:03.676 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

02:03.756 --> 02:06.226 align:middle
the pace of improvement in the
labor market has moderated.

02:07.186 --> 02:09.936 align:middle
The unemployment rate declined
over the past five months

02:10.276 --> 02:13.976 align:middle
but remained elevated at
7.9 percent as of September.

02:15.426 --> 02:17.806 align:middle
Although we welcome this
progress, we will not lose sight

02:17.886 --> 02:20.496 align:middle
of the millions of Americans
who remain out of work.

02:21.876 --> 02:25.466 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

02:25.876 --> 02:28.406 align:middle
and those least able to
shoulder the burden have been

02:28.406 --> 02:29.056 align:middle
hardest hit.

02:29.536 --> 02:31.966 align:middle
In particular, the high level

02:31.966 --> 02:34.446 align:middle
of joblessness has
been especially severe

02:34.446 --> 02:37.816 align:middle
for lower-wage workers in the
services sector, for women,

02:38.286 --> 02:40.456 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

02:41.846 --> 02:45.046 align:middle
The economic dislocation
has upended many lives

02:45.046 --> 02:47.276 align:middle
and created great
uncertainty about the future.

02:48.956 --> 02:51.596 align:middle
The pandemic has also
left a significant imprint

02:51.596 --> 02:52.426 align:middle
on inflation.

02:53.426 --> 02:56.756 align:middle
Following large declines in the
spring, consumer prices picked

02:56.756 --> 02:59.526 align:middle
up over the summer, in
part reflecting a rise

02:59.526 --> 03:00.836 align:middle
in durable goods prices.

03:01.606 --> 03:04.516 align:middle
However, for those sectors
that have been most affected

03:04.516 --> 03:07.306 align:middle
by the pandemic, prices
remain particularly soft.

03:08.176 --> 03:09.976 align:middle
Overall, on a 12-month basis,

03:09.976 --> 03:13.636 align:middle
inflation remains below our 2
percent longer-run objective.

03:14.286 --> 03:19.136 align:middle
As we have emphasized
throughout the pandemic,

03:19.246 --> 03:22.006 align:middle
the outlook for the economy
is extraordinarily uncertain

03:22.566 --> 03:25.486 align:middle
and will depend in large part
on the success of efforts

03:25.486 --> 03:26.766 align:middle
to keep the virus in check.

03:27.896 --> 03:30.286 align:middle
The recent rise in
new COVID-19 cases,

03:30.336 --> 03:32.446 align:middle
both here in the United
States and abroad,

03:32.896 --> 03:34.446 align:middle
is particularly concerning.

03:35.596 --> 03:37.066 align:middle
All of us have a role to play

03:37.066 --> 03:38.996 align:middle
in our nation's response
to the pandemic.

03:39.776 --> 03:42.196 align:middle
Following the advice of
public health professionals

03:42.196 --> 03:45.336 align:middle
to keep appropriate social
distances and to wear masks

03:45.336 --> 03:48.676 align:middle
in public will help get the
economy back to full strength.

03:50.116 --> 03:52.496 align:middle
A full economic recovery
is unlikely

03:52.576 --> 03:55.686 align:middle
until people are confident
that it's safe to reengage

03:55.686 --> 03:57.346 align:middle
in a broad range of activities.

04:00.296 --> 04:03.146 align:middle
The Federal Reserve's response
to this crisis has been guided

04:03.146 --> 04:05.486 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:05.486 --> 04:07.356 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people,

04:07.686 --> 04:10.246 align:middle
along with our responsibilities
to promote the stability

04:10.246 --> 04:11.236 align:middle
of the financial system.

04:12.216 --> 04:14.536 align:middle
As noted in our Statement
on Longer-Run Goals

04:14.536 --> 04:18.036 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy,
we view maximum employment as a

04:18.036 --> 04:19.976 align:middle
"broad-based and
inclusive goal."

04:20.876 --> 04:22.936 align:middle
Our ability to achieve
maximum employment

04:22.936 --> 04:24.866 align:middle
in the years ahead
depends importantly

04:25.336 --> 04:28.676 align:middle
on having longer-term inflation
expectations well anchored

04:28.676 --> 04:29.436 align:middle
at 2 percent.

04:30.036 --> 04:32.986 align:middle
As we said in September
and again today,

04:33.466 --> 04:36.026 align:middle
with inflation running
persistently below 2 percent,

04:36.236 --> 04:39.626 align:middle
we will aim to achieve inflation
moderately above 2 percent

04:39.626 --> 04:43.796 align:middle
for some time so that inflation
averages 2 percent over time

04:43.796 --> 04:46.776 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

04:46.776 --> 04:47.896 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

04:49.146 --> 04:51.306 align:middle
We expect to maintain
an accommodative stance

04:51.306 --> 04:53.486 align:middle
of monetary policy
until these employment

04:53.486 --> 04:55.566 align:middle
and inflation outcomes
are achieved.

04:56.736 --> 05:01.096 align:middle
With regard to interest
rates, we continue to expect

05:01.096 --> 05:04.026 align:middle
that it will be appropriate
to maintain the current 0

05:04.026 --> 05:06.766 align:middle
to ¼ percent target range
for the federal funds rate

05:07.376 --> 05:10.166 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

05:10.166 --> 05:12.266 align:middle
with the Committee's assessments
of maximum employment

05:12.856 --> 05:15.866 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

05:15.866 --> 05:18.596 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

05:19.056 --> 05:22.016 align:middle
In addition, over coming
months we will continue

05:22.016 --> 05:24.436 align:middle
to increase our holdings
of Treasury securities

05:24.436 --> 05:26.396 align:middle
and agency mortgage-backed
securities

05:26.396 --> 05:27.746 align:middle
at least at the current pace.

05:29.056 --> 05:30.986 align:middle
These asset purchases
are intended

05:30.986 --> 05:32.936 align:middle
to sustain smooth
market functioning

05:33.216 --> 05:35.816 align:middle
and help foster accommodative
financial conditions,

05:36.326 --> 05:38.066 align:middle
thereby supporting
the flow of credit

05:38.066 --> 05:39.316 align:middle
to households and businesses.

05:40.316 --> 05:41.536 align:middle
At this meeting, my colleagues

05:41.536 --> 05:43.736 align:middle
and I discussed our
asset purchases

05:43.926 --> 05:46.246 align:middle
and the role they are playing
in supporting the recovery.

05:46.786 --> 05:49.636 align:middle
At the current pace,
our holdings

05:49.946 --> 05:52.386 align:middle
of securities are rising
at a substantial rate

05:52.386 --> 05:57.476 align:middle
of $120 billion per month-$80
billion per month of Treasuries

05:57.536 --> 06:00.606 align:middle
and $40 billion per
month of agency MBS.

06:01.656 --> 06:02.906 align:middle
We believe these purchases,

06:02.906 --> 06:04.906 align:middle
along with the very
large purchases made

06:04.906 --> 06:07.806 align:middle
to preserve financial stability
in the depths of the crisis,

06:08.356 --> 06:10.686 align:middle
have materially eased
financial conditions

06:11.116 --> 06:13.676 align:middle
and are providing substantial
support to the economy.

06:15.086 --> 06:18.096 align:middle
Looking ahead, we will
continue to monitor developments

06:18.096 --> 06:22.086 align:middle
and assess how our ongoing asset
purchases can best support our

06:22.086 --> 06:24.966 align:middle
maximum-employment and
price-stability objectives

06:25.386 --> 06:27.776 align:middle
as well as market functioning
and financial stability.

06:29.656 --> 06:32.106 align:middle
The Federal Reserve has
also been taking broad

06:32.106 --> 06:34.816 align:middle
and forceful actions to more
directly support the flow

06:34.816 --> 06:38.056 align:middle
of credit in the economy for
households, for businesses large

06:38.056 --> 06:40.636 align:middle
and small, and for state
and local governments.

06:42.096 --> 06:45.386 align:middle
Preserving the flow of credit is
essential for mitigating damage

06:45.386 --> 06:47.886 align:middle
to the economy and
promoting a robust recovery.

06:49.236 --> 06:52.246 align:middle
Many of our programs rely
on emergency lending powers

06:52.246 --> 06:54.396 align:middle
that require the support
of the Treasury Department

06:54.536 --> 06:57.696 align:middle
and are available only in
very unusual circumstances,

06:58.116 --> 07:00.246 align:middle
such as those we find
ourselves in today.

07:01.736 --> 07:05.146 align:middle
These programs serve as a
backstop to key credit markets

07:05.606 --> 07:07.416 align:middle
and have helped to
restore the flow of credit

07:07.416 --> 07:09.596 align:middle
from private lenders
through normal channels.

07:09.676 --> 07:12.476 align:middle
We have deployed
these lending powers

07:12.476 --> 07:15.566 align:middle
to an unprecedented extent,
enabled in large part

07:15.566 --> 07:18.606 align:middle
by financial backing and support
from Congress and the Treasury.

07:19.836 --> 07:22.606 align:middle
When the time comes, after
the crisis has passed,

07:23.016 --> 07:26.316 align:middle
we will put these emergency
tools back in the toolbox.

07:26.836 --> 07:29.256 align:middle
As I have emphasized before,

07:29.766 --> 07:32.486 align:middle
these are lending powers,
not spending powers.

07:33.046 --> 07:36.076 align:middle
The Fed cannot grant money
to particular beneficiaries.

07:36.646 --> 07:38.956 align:middle
We can only create
programs or facilities

07:38.956 --> 07:42.976 align:middle
with broad-based eligibility to
make loans to solvent entities

07:42.976 --> 07:45.416 align:middle
with the expectation that
the loans will be repaid.

07:46.386 --> 07:48.826 align:middle
Many borrowers are
benefiting from these programs,

07:48.926 --> 07:50.496 align:middle
as is the overall economy.

07:51.346 --> 07:53.866 align:middle
But for many others, getting
a loan that may be difficult

07:53.866 --> 07:55.426 align:middle
to repay may not be the answer.

07:56.576 --> 07:59.116 align:middle
In these cases, direct
fiscal support may be needed.

07:59.936 --> 08:02.196 align:middle
Elected officials have
the power to tax and spend

08:02.196 --> 08:04.676 align:middle
and to make decisions about
where we, as a society,

08:05.006 --> 08:07.006 align:middle
should direct our
collective resources.

08:07.506 --> 08:09.426 align:middle
The fiscal policy actions

08:09.426 --> 08:12.016 align:middle
that have been taken thus far
have made a critical difference

08:12.016 --> 08:14.976 align:middle
to families, businesses, and
communities across the country.

08:16.596 --> 08:20.206 align:middle
Even so, the current economic
downturn is the most severe

08:20.206 --> 08:21.086 align:middle
in our lifetimes.

08:21.716 --> 08:23.756 align:middle
It will take a while to
get back to the levels

08:23.756 --> 08:26.166 align:middle
of economic activity and
employment that prevailed

08:26.166 --> 08:27.326 align:middle
at the beginning of this year,

08:28.066 --> 08:30.656 align:middle
and it may take continued
support from both monetary

08:30.656 --> 08:32.356 align:middle
and fiscal policy
to achieve that.

08:32.856 --> 08:37.386 align:middle
I'd like to mention a couple of
changes that we plan on making

08:37.386 --> 08:39.586 align:middle
to our Summary of Economic
Projections beginning

08:39.586 --> 08:40.176 align:middle
in December.

08:40.856 --> 08:44.806 align:middle
First, we will release the
entire package of SEP materials

08:45.296 --> 08:48.776 align:middle
at the same time that the
FOMC statement comes out.

08:49.566 --> 08:52.576 align:middle
Previously, some of these
materials were released three

08:52.576 --> 08:56.066 align:middle
weeks after the meeting
as part of the minutes.

08:56.066 --> 08:58.126 align:middle
This step will make more
information available

08:58.126 --> 08:59.726 align:middle
at the time of our
policy announcements,

09:00.206 --> 09:02.356 align:middle
including the distributions
of forecasts

09:02.726 --> 09:05.636 align:middle
and how participants judge
the uncertainty and risks

09:05.636 --> 09:07.016 align:middle
that attend their projections.

09:08.056 --> 09:11.656 align:middle
Second, we will add two new
graphs that show how the balance

09:11.656 --> 09:13.476 align:middle
of participants'
assessments of uncertainty

09:13.476 --> 09:15.256 align:middle
and risks have evolved
over time.

09:16.326 --> 09:19.136 align:middle
These changes to the SEP will
provide a timely perspective

09:19.136 --> 09:21.536 align:middle
on the risks and uncertainties
that surround the modal

09:21.536 --> 09:24.946 align:middle
or baseline projections,
thereby highlighting some

09:24.946 --> 09:26.666 align:middle
of the risk-management
considerations

09:26.666 --> 09:28.356 align:middle
that are relevant
for monetary policy.

09:29.776 --> 09:33.266 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

09:33.336 --> 09:35.666 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

09:36.546 --> 09:39.216 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

09:40.026 --> 09:42.466 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

09:42.466 --> 09:45.826 align:middle
to support the economy and to
help assure that the recovery

09:45.826 --> 09:49.706 align:middle
from this difficult period
will be as robust as possible.

09:50.466 --> 09:50.886 align:middle
Thank you.

09:51.096 --> 09:53.726 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

09:53.816 --> 09:55.006 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:57.406 --> 09:58.426 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

10:00.656 --> 10:01.496 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

10:01.496 --> 10:03.066 align:middle
It's Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

10:03.066 --> 10:05.166 align:middle
Thanks very much for
taking my question.

10:05.196 --> 10:07.516 align:middle
I'm wondering if you
can speak specifically

10:07.516 --> 10:10.776 align:middle
about what indicators you're
seeing that suggest the pace

10:10.776 --> 10:13.826 align:middle
of improvement has moderated,
including in the labor market,

10:13.826 --> 10:16.226 align:middle
and how correlated
those indicators are

10:16.226 --> 10:18.106 align:middle
to the recent rise
in COVID cases

10:18.106 --> 10:19.796 align:middle
that we've seen going
into flu season.

10:19.796 --> 10:20.416 align:middle
Thank you.

10:20.496 --> 10:21.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:21.136 --> 10:22.776 align:middle
Sure. So, of course.

10:23.076 --> 10:24.406 align:middle
Let's start with February.

10:24.406 --> 10:27.026 align:middle
In February, we had an economy
that was performing well.

10:27.576 --> 10:31.606 align:middle
Then the pandemic hit, and we
had a record decline in activity

10:31.606 --> 10:34.736 align:middle
in March and April, and then
we had a record bounceback

10:34.796 --> 10:35.736 align:middle
in May and June.

10:36.306 --> 10:39.276 align:middle
And so as I think would have
been expected and was expected,

10:39.586 --> 10:42.506 align:middle
the pace of improvement from
May and June has now moderated,

10:42.506 --> 10:43.756 align:middle
so it's not unexpected.

10:44.096 --> 10:47.566 align:middle
And I think if you look at
just about anything, you know,

10:48.086 --> 10:52.626 align:middle
for example, the payroll
readings, the payroll job gains

10:52.626 --> 10:55.106 align:middle
in May and June were
just outsized.

10:55.426 --> 10:58.226 align:middle
And they're certainly still very
large, but they've-but the pace

10:58.226 --> 10:59.696 align:middle
of improvement has moderated.

11:00.376 --> 11:03.526 align:middle
That's the case for, for
all different measures

11:03.526 --> 11:06.306 align:middle
in the labor force-in
the labor market, rather.

11:06.306 --> 11:07.726 align:middle
Another would be claims.

11:07.726 --> 11:10.726 align:middle
Just about all the data
were-showed a big bounceback.

11:10.726 --> 11:12.686 align:middle
But then, as you would
expect, when, when you sort

11:12.686 --> 11:15.336 align:middle
of had people-a lot of people
go back to work at once,

11:15.336 --> 11:16.636 align:middle
the pace will moderate.

11:17.066 --> 11:18.586 align:middle
Same thing with economic
activity.

11:18.586 --> 11:23.156 align:middle
Most forecasts call for, you
know, still a significant growth

11:23.156 --> 11:26.546 align:middle
in the fourth quarter but not at
the 33 percent annualized pace

11:26.546 --> 11:28.226 align:middle
that we had in the
third quarter.

11:28.536 --> 11:31.406 align:middle
So, in a sense, that would
be-that would be as expected.

11:31.976 --> 11:37.116 align:middle
We have been concerned that the
downside risks, though, are,

11:37.216 --> 11:40.136 align:middle
are prevalent now, which
are-which are really the risk

11:40.136 --> 11:43.836 align:middle
of the further spread of the
disease and also the risk that,

11:43.916 --> 11:47.966 align:middle
that households will run through
the savings they've managed

11:47.966 --> 11:49.896 align:middle
to accumulate on their
balance sheet and that

11:49.896 --> 11:51.006 align:middle
that could weigh on activity.

11:51.216 --> 11:53.246 align:middle
But what we see up to
the present, really, is,

11:53.596 --> 11:57.136 align:middle
continued growth,
continued expansion, but at,

11:57.136 --> 11:59.166 align:middle
at a gradually moderating pace.

11:59.506 --> 11:59.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:04.716 --> 12:05.196 align:middle
Thank you.

12:05.366 --> 12:08.436 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

12:08.776 --> 12:11.556 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

12:11.556 --> 12:12.006 align:middle
Thank you.

12:12.006 --> 12:13.046 align:middle
Could you talk a little bit

12:13.046 --> 12:17.716 align:middle
about where you think
the stimulus package

12:17.716 --> 12:20.306 align:middle
that is being debated
in Congress is

12:20.306 --> 12:24.516 align:middle
and how severe a threat
that could be to the economy

12:24.516 --> 12:28.026 align:middle
if it does not get passed,
say, before January?

12:28.026 --> 12:29.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:29.796 --> 12:33.566 align:middle
So it is-obviously, it's for
Congress to decide the timing,

12:33.566 --> 12:36.586 align:middle
size, and components of further
fiscal support for the economy,

12:37.376 --> 12:39.646 align:middle
and I will say that
the support provided

12:39.646 --> 12:42.506 align:middle
by the CARES Act was
absolutely essential

12:42.506 --> 12:44.636 align:middle
in supporting the recovery
that we've seen so far,

12:45.006 --> 12:47.106 align:middle
which has generally
exceeded expectations.

12:47.636 --> 12:50.596 align:middle
And I do think it's likely
that further support is likely

12:50.596 --> 12:54.086 align:middle
to be needed for monetary
policy and fiscal policy.

12:54.086 --> 12:56.966 align:middle
I just mentioned the two risks
that I think we, we face,

12:56.966 --> 12:58.386 align:middle
and those would be
well addressed

12:59.026 --> 13:00.146 align:middle
through more fiscal policy.

13:00.146 --> 13:01.696 align:middle
One is the further
spread of the virus,

13:01.696 --> 13:05.206 align:middle
and the other is the lapsing
of the CARES Act benefits

13:05.206 --> 13:07.586 align:middle
and the savings on
people's balance sheets

13:07.586 --> 13:08.216 align:middle
that will dwindle.

13:08.726 --> 13:13.696 align:middle
But I, you know, I think it's
appropriate for us not to try

13:13.696 --> 13:15.926 align:middle
to prescribe for Congress
exactly what they should do

13:15.926 --> 13:17.846 align:middle
or what the timing of it
should be or what the size

13:17.846 --> 13:20.746 align:middle
of it should be and
leave it at that.

13:23.666 --> 13:24.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:24.066 --> 13:27.766 align:middle
Nick Timiraos.

13:27.766 --> 13:30.096 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:30.096 --> 13:30.856 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

13:30.856 --> 13:32.736 align:middle
Nick Timiraos at the
Wall Street Journal.

13:33.116 --> 13:36.656 align:middle
To follow on Marty's question,
you really have been saying

13:36.656 --> 13:40.296 align:middle
since April that more is needed
on the fiscal policy front,

13:40.296 --> 13:43.056 align:middle
and yet we don't seem to be
that much closer than we were

13:43.056 --> 13:46.196 align:middle
in the spring or the summer
to additional spending.

13:46.806 --> 13:50.926 align:middle
Two questions: Would the lack
of fiscal support compel the Fed

13:51.496 --> 13:55.096 align:middle
to provide additional
accommodation, and are you

13:55.096 --> 13:58.706 align:middle
and your colleagues being
more vocal about the need

13:58.706 --> 14:02.186 align:middle
for fiscal support because the
capacity for monetary policy

14:02.186 --> 14:05.436 align:middle
to support growth is diminished
here, given the low level

14:05.436 --> 14:06.816 align:middle
of short- and long-term rates?

14:07.066 --> 14:07.706 align:middle
Thank you.

14:08.906 --> 14:09.196 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:09.196 --> 14:10.616 align:middle
So on your first
question, you know,

14:10.616 --> 14:14.116 align:middle
we'll take into account
all external factors and,

14:14.116 --> 14:16.136 align:middle
and do what we think we
need to do with the tools

14:16.136 --> 14:19.686 align:middle
that we have to pursue
our goals.

14:19.826 --> 14:20.796 align:middle
That's what we will do.

14:21.096 --> 14:22.726 align:middle
And I've said it on
a couple of occasions

14:22.726 --> 14:25.476 align:middle
that that will go better
and move more quickly

14:25.476 --> 14:29.106 align:middle
if we have a broad
set of policies

14:29.106 --> 14:30.186 align:middle
from across the government.

14:30.286 --> 14:32.226 align:middle
And we've said this in the
very beginning: It's really,

14:32.656 --> 14:34.496 align:middle
first and foremost,
health-care policy,

14:34.496 --> 14:38.156 align:middle
getting the-getting the spread
of the virus under control

14:38.156 --> 14:41.256 align:middle
and working on therapeutics and
vaccines and that kind of thing,

14:41.256 --> 14:43.776 align:middle
getting-so that-those
are absolutely critical

14:43.776 --> 14:44.576 align:middle
to the economy.

14:44.576 --> 14:47.276 align:middle
Now, those are important,
as well as health policies.

14:47.276 --> 14:48.846 align:middle
They're, they're going to
be critical to the economy.

14:48.846 --> 14:50.876 align:middle
Fiscal policy can
do what we can't,

14:50.876 --> 14:54.706 align:middle
which is to replace lost incomes
for people who are out of work

14:54.706 --> 14:55.626 align:middle
through no fault of their own.

14:55.626 --> 14:57.016 align:middle
And then we-what we can do is,

14:57.396 --> 14:59.796 align:middle
we can obviously support
financial stability

15:00.156 --> 15:02.476 align:middle
through our lending
programs, and,

15:02.476 --> 15:05.936 align:middle
and we can support demand
through, through interest rates

15:05.936 --> 15:08.546 align:middle
and asset purchases
and that sort of thing.

15:08.656 --> 15:12.736 align:middle
So we're, we're going to
take the economy as it comes,

15:12.736 --> 15:14.306 align:middle
including all external factors.

15:14.826 --> 15:20.606 align:middle
So I think all of us lived
through the experience

15:21.396 --> 15:24.206 align:middle
of the-of the years after
the Global Financial Crisis.

15:24.276 --> 15:29.376 align:middle
And for a number of years there,
in the middle of the recovery,

15:29.966 --> 15:32.486 align:middle
fiscal policy was pretty tight.

15:32.486 --> 15:34.146 align:middle
And. and I think
I just would say

15:34.146 --> 15:36.606 align:middle
that I think we'll have
a stronger recovery

15:37.046 --> 15:39.226 align:middle
if we can just get at least
some more fiscal support

15:39.286 --> 15:41.946 align:middle
when it's appropriate, you
know, when it's appropriate

15:41.946 --> 15:43.906 align:middle
and in the size Congress
thinks is appropriate.

15:44.406 --> 15:47.856 align:middle
I do think that that will
likely-and, by the way, you see,

15:47.996 --> 15:51.206 align:middle
you know, a lot of discussion
on both sides of the aisle,

15:51.206 --> 15:54.386 align:middle
on both sides of the Hill
that suggests generally

15:54.386 --> 15:55.356 align:middle
that there will be something.

15:55.776 --> 16:00.076 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:00.076 --> 16:00.566 align:middle
Thank you.

16:00.566 --> 16:02.116 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

16:03.636 --> 16:04.686 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

16:04.686 --> 16:05.046 align:middle
Thank you.

16:05.046 --> 16:06.946 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, also
to follow up on sort

16:06.946 --> 16:08.736 align:middle
of what Nick was talking
about, two questions

16:08.736 --> 16:10.436 align:middle
about quantitative easing.

16:10.436 --> 16:12.966 align:middle
The first is, if the market
is functioning better,

16:12.966 --> 16:15.956 align:middle
as you and other Fed
officials have said,

16:15.956 --> 16:19.366 align:middle
and QE right now is designed
for smooth market functioning,

16:19.706 --> 16:22.286 align:middle
why haven't you reduced
QE that you're doing

16:22.286 --> 16:24.076 align:middle
if the market is
functioning better already?

16:24.076 --> 16:28.726 align:middle
The second question
I have is, what good,

16:28.796 --> 16:32.306 align:middle
for the broader economy, would
additional QE do at this point,

16:32.756 --> 16:36.546 align:middle
given that interest rates are
already low and don't seem

16:36.546 --> 16:39.406 align:middle
to be rising even
above 1 percent?

16:39.656 --> 16:40.476 align:middle
Thank you.

16:40.606 --> 16:41.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:41.166 --> 16:44.156 align:middle
So, on the first-the
first question,

16:45.586 --> 16:49.906 align:middle
it's-our asset purchases
are serving both purchases,

16:49.906 --> 16:52.796 align:middle
both-sorry, both purposes:
financial market function

16:52.796 --> 16:54.436 align:middle
and support for economic
activity.

16:54.926 --> 16:58.816 align:middle
So-and that's really been true-I
think in the very beginning

16:58.816 --> 17:02.156 align:middle
of the crisis, the main focus
was, obviously, financial,

17:02.336 --> 17:05.966 align:middle
financial market function,
particularly in, in, you know,

17:05.966 --> 17:07.176 align:middle
some of the major markets.

17:07.216 --> 17:10.936 align:middle
But after that period, we've,
we've understood all along

17:10.936 --> 17:13.726 align:middle
that our purchases are also
supporting economic activity,

17:13.726 --> 17:14.426 align:middle
and that's important.

17:14.426 --> 17:16.146 align:middle
And that need hasn't
dwindled at all.

17:16.146 --> 17:19.706 align:middle
So, so we haven't looked
at reducing purchases.

17:20.456 --> 17:23.636 align:middle
So in terms of what they can
do, first I would just say,

17:24.076 --> 17:25.156 align:middle
the purchases that we have

17:25.156 --> 17:28.406 align:middle
in place are providing
strong support

17:28.696 --> 17:30.296 align:middle
to economic activity still.

17:30.296 --> 17:33.326 align:middle
And, by the way, they're
sustaining the gains we've made

17:33.326 --> 17:34.426 align:middle
in financial stability.

17:34.706 --> 17:38.476 align:middle
You know, we don't-we don't
take anything for granted.

17:38.596 --> 17:42.826 align:middle
We, we don't expect that
things will deteriorate.

17:42.826 --> 17:44.736 align:middle
But, nonetheless,
we have a habit

17:44.736 --> 17:47.176 align:middle
of keeping things in
place for a while.

17:47.176 --> 17:51.176 align:middle
So we're not taking our gains
in financial market function

17:51.236 --> 17:52.786 align:middle
for granted, although,
admittedly,

17:52.786 --> 17:53.936 align:middle
they've been very large.

17:55.146 --> 17:59.716 align:middle
So the asset purchases are just
another very important piece

18:00.096 --> 18:03.506 align:middle
of the accommodative
policy stance that we have.

18:03.506 --> 18:04.816 align:middle
And, you know, as you know,

18:04.816 --> 18:07.166 align:middle
these-we're buying
$120 billion a month.

18:07.166 --> 18:10.996 align:middle
That's $1.44 trillion, if
I remember my times tables.

18:11.526 --> 18:14.696 align:middle
And it's just providing
a lot of support for,

18:14.696 --> 18:17.896 align:middle
for economic activity and,
by the way, removing just

18:17.896 --> 18:19.966 align:middle
about the same amount
of duration risk

18:19.966 --> 18:21.746 align:middle
from private hands as QE3 did.

18:22.156 --> 18:25.746 align:middle
So this is a big program,
and it's doing a lot of good.

18:25.746 --> 18:29.366 align:middle
And we also, today, you know,
we had a full discussion

18:29.366 --> 18:30.166 align:middle
of the options

18:30.166 --> 18:32.936 align:middle
around quantitative
ease-not quantitative easing,

18:32.936 --> 18:37.686 align:middle
the asset purchase program, and,
you know, we understand the,

18:37.686 --> 18:40.986 align:middle
the ways in which we can
adjust the parameters of it

18:40.986 --> 18:44.186 align:middle
to deliver more accommodation if
it turns out to be appropriate.

18:44.326 --> 18:47.486 align:middle
Right now, we think
that this very large,

18:47.486 --> 18:49.616 align:middle
effective program is delivering
about the right amount

18:49.616 --> 18:50.836 align:middle
of accommodation and support

18:50.836 --> 18:53.216 align:middle
for the markets,
and so it continues.

18:53.346 --> 18:53.826 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:57.306 --> 18:58.256 align:middle
Thank you.

18:58.396 --> 18:59.556 align:middle
Craig Torres, Bloomberg.

19:02.136 --> 19:03.696 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

19:03.946 --> 19:04.676 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

19:05.006 --> 19:07.286 align:middle
First, it's great
to see you're okay.

19:07.286 --> 19:14.106 align:middle
Chair Powell, what is the
risk that we come out of this

19:14.106 --> 19:18.416 align:middle
with lower productivity;
weaker labor force attachment;

19:19.066 --> 19:23.456 align:middle
slower growth; and, important
for your tools, Chair Powell,

19:23.456 --> 19:25.626 align:middle
a cycle of lower
real interest rates?

19:25.696 --> 19:29.216 align:middle
And if you think this type
of scarring is a risk,

19:30.126 --> 19:32.576 align:middle
then I'm wondering, what's
stopping the Federal Reserve

19:32.576 --> 19:35.626 align:middle
from having a more explicit
dialogue with Congress

19:36.026 --> 19:39.816 align:middle
about the particular types of
fiscal support we might need

19:39.816 --> 19:43.646 align:middle
to avoid this outcome to
make sure that you push away

19:43.646 --> 19:46.476 align:middle
from the zero boundary
or that the economy does

19:47.066 --> 19:48.276 align:middle
when we exit this?

19:48.276 --> 19:49.836 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

19:49.836 --> 19:50.156 align:middle
Thank you.

19:50.156 --> 19:56.196 align:middle
So you did-you laid out nicely
the, the risks of damage

19:56.196 --> 19:57.576 align:middle
to the supply side
of the economy,

19:57.576 --> 19:58.896 align:middle
or "scarring," as you put it.

19:59.356 --> 20:00.696 align:middle
And we've been talking
about those

20:00.696 --> 20:03.556 align:middle
since the very beginning
of the pandemic.

20:03.686 --> 20:05.336 align:middle
The risk is that, for example,

20:05.336 --> 20:07.566 align:middle
people are out of
the labor force

20:07.566 --> 20:08.866 align:middle
for an extended period of time.

20:08.866 --> 20:10.966 align:middle
They lose their attachment
to the labor force,

20:11.516 --> 20:13.036 align:middle
and it's harder to get back.

20:13.036 --> 20:14.186 align:middle
Your skills atrophy.

20:14.186 --> 20:15.166 align:middle
It's harder to get back in.

20:15.166 --> 20:17.896 align:middle
And the record is, if you don't
get back in very fairly quickly,

20:17.896 --> 20:19.226 align:middle
it can be harder to get back in.

20:19.616 --> 20:21.296 align:middle
And that holds down
the whole economy.

20:21.296 --> 20:24.526 align:middle
And, by the way, it also-it
places enormous burdens

20:24.526 --> 20:27.566 align:middle
on individuals who may
have this happen to them

20:27.756 --> 20:29.956 align:middle
at an important stage
in their career.

20:29.956 --> 20:32.496 align:middle
So it's, it's, it's important.

20:32.496 --> 20:35.886 align:middle
And, you know-so that is one of
the reasons why our response was

20:35.886 --> 20:37.886 align:middle
so strong and so
urgent at the beginning

20:37.886 --> 20:40.246 align:middle
and why we called this,
this set of risks out.

20:40.246 --> 20:41.896 align:middle
I don't know how we
could be much more vocal

20:41.896 --> 20:42.876 align:middle
about it than we have been.

20:43.286 --> 20:47.996 align:middle
Fortunately, for-you know, the
economic recovery has exceeded,

20:47.996 --> 20:50.796 align:middle
certainly, the downside cases
that we were very concerned

20:50.796 --> 20:53.006 align:middle
about and even, even
exceeded-sort

20:53.006 --> 20:55.456 align:middle
of exceeded the baseline
expectations.

20:56.086 --> 20:57.676 align:middle
Now, that's so far.

20:58.136 --> 21:00.356 align:middle
We are a long way
from our goals.

21:00.436 --> 21:03.596 align:middle
And, you know, we're,
we're sort of halfway there

21:03.596 --> 21:05.596 align:middle
on the labor market
recovery at best.

21:06.176 --> 21:09.266 align:middle
And, and there are parts of
the economy where it's going

21:09.266 --> 21:11.676 align:middle
to be hard until there's
a vaccine-you know,

21:11.676 --> 21:13.516 align:middle
the parts of the economy.

21:13.976 --> 21:16.306 align:middle
So, you know, that's
the supply-side damage.

21:16.306 --> 21:18.306 align:middle
That's sort of the third
thing that we talk about.

21:18.306 --> 21:19.396 align:middle
The first, at the beginning,

21:19.396 --> 21:22.146 align:middle
it was to provide
relief and comfort.

21:22.406 --> 21:24.366 align:middle
The second-then the
second part was

21:24.366 --> 21:26.976 align:middle
to provide support when-to
the expansion when it came.

21:27.166 --> 21:29.376 align:middle
And the third was to
avoid longer-run damage

21:29.376 --> 21:31.736 align:middle
to the economy, and that's,
that's all of these things.

21:32.246 --> 21:33.566 align:middle
I think we have been vocal.

21:33.566 --> 21:35.496 align:middle
You know, we'll, we'll try
to continue to do that.

21:35.496 --> 21:38.116 align:middle
And, and those are-that's
again-that goes

21:38.116 --> 21:41.336 align:middle
to keeping this episode
as short as it can be

21:41.336 --> 21:44.726 align:middle
and avoiding unnecessary
business bankruptcies,

21:44.726 --> 21:46.476 align:middle
unnecessary household
bankruptcies,

21:47.066 --> 21:49.826 align:middle
and unnecessary long-term
stays of unemployment

21:49.826 --> 21:51.436 align:middle
or supporting people
through them

21:51.996 --> 21:54.386 align:middle
so that they can maintain
their financial footing

21:54.386 --> 21:56.376 align:middle
and their lives and
be able to go back

21:56.376 --> 21:57.706 align:middle
to work in a productive way.

21:58.236 --> 22:00.976 align:middle
It's, it's very important
that-you know,

22:00.976 --> 22:03.786 align:middle
there is a real threat here
of those things, and, and,

22:03.786 --> 22:06.196 align:middle
you know, we're trying
to do everything we can

22:06.196 --> 22:07.156 align:middle
to minimize that threat.

22:07.156 --> 22:07.796 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:29.466 --> 22:30.106 align:middle
[Inaudible] JEANNA SMIALEK.

22:30.106 --> 22:30.346 align:middle
[Inaudible] -look like

22:30.346 --> 22:32.786 align:middle
and whether you anticipate
any consequences

22:32.786 --> 22:34.116 align:middle
if they are not extended?

22:35.526 --> 22:35.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:35.846 --> 22:36.406 align:middle
Yes, thanks.

22:36.596 --> 22:39.156 align:middle
So, first, let me say, we do
think that the facilities have,

22:39.156 --> 22:42.446 align:middle
have generally served their
purposes well, particularly

22:42.606 --> 22:45.086 align:middle
in supporting the flow of
credit, particularly acting

22:45.086 --> 22:46.666 align:middle
as backstops to private markets.

22:46.666 --> 22:47.786 align:middle
So, overall, we think that the,

22:47.786 --> 22:49.536 align:middle
the programs have,
have gone well.

22:50.686 --> 22:52.016 align:middle
In terms of the extension,
we are,

22:52.016 --> 22:54.316 align:middle
we are just now turning
to that question.

22:54.316 --> 22:56.996 align:middle
You know, we've had a lot of
things to work our way through.

22:56.996 --> 22:58.776 align:middle
Right now we're just in
the process of turning

22:58.776 --> 23:01.516 align:middle
to that question and, of
course, not made any decisions.

23:01.516 --> 23:03.836 align:middle
And in terms of the process,
this is a decision that,

23:03.836 --> 23:06.286 align:middle
of course, we have to
make and will make jointly

23:06.316 --> 23:07.426 align:middle
with the Treasury Department.

23:07.516 --> 23:07.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:11.896 --> 23:12.246 align:middle
Thank you.

23:12.246 --> 23:12.676 align:middle
David Gura.

23:12.676 --> 23:13.096 align:middle
DAVID GURA.

23:13.096 --> 23:20.486 align:middle
[Inaudible] IMF Chief Economist
Gita Gopinath wrote-Gita

23:20.486 --> 23:22.606 align:middle
Gopinath wrote a piece this
week in which she said,

23:22.606 --> 23:25.276 align:middle
unequivocally, we're in a
global liquidity trap and talked

23:25.276 --> 23:28.446 align:middle
about the limits of
monetary policy right now.

23:28.446 --> 23:30.266 align:middle
She said, "Fiscal
policy will need

23:30.266 --> 23:32.616 align:middle
to be the main game in town."

23:32.616 --> 23:33.846 align:middle
And it's been a busy week.

23:33.846 --> 23:35.976 align:middle
I don't know if you were able to
pick up the pink paper and read

23:35.976 --> 23:39.066 align:middle
that piece, but she
does say 97 percent

23:39.066 --> 23:41.306 align:middle
of advanced economies have
rates below 1 percent.

23:41.306 --> 23:43.266 align:middle
And I just wonder if you'd
agree with her, in principle,

23:43.656 --> 23:46.086 align:middle
that we are in a
global liquidity trap,

23:46.086 --> 23:48.446 align:middle
and what the consequences
of that would be.

23:48.446 --> 23:50.346 align:middle
We've talked a lot about fiscal
policy here domestically,

23:50.346 --> 23:51.926 align:middle
and she talks about
the need for sort

23:52.136 --> 23:55.256 align:middle
of a global cohesive
approach to, to fiscal policy.

23:55.776 --> 23:56.886 align:middle
My second question is, you talk

23:56.886 --> 23:59.076 align:middle
about how you follow
the epidemiology.

23:59.076 --> 24:01.916 align:middle
You're, you're looking
[inaudible].

24:01.996 --> 24:10.316 align:middle
And I wanted to ask
you about how you look

24:10.316 --> 24:11.846 align:middle
at [inaudible] Christine
Lagarde laying

24:11.846 --> 24:13.456 align:middle
out what the ECB plans to do.

24:14.026 --> 24:16.336 align:middle
As you lent an ear to what she
had to say, the dire warnings

24:16.336 --> 24:18.956 align:middle
that she was making this morning
as she looks at the situation

24:18.956 --> 24:21.956 align:middle
that Europe's in, what can
you and your colleagues learn

24:21.956 --> 24:25.716 align:middle
about the second wave or the
third wave that you've talked

24:25.716 --> 24:27.136 align:middle
about and, and we've
all feared when it comes

24:27.136 --> 24:29.326 align:middle
to a, a policy response?

24:29.776 --> 24:30.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:30.066 --> 24:30.476 align:middle
Okay, thanks.

24:30.476 --> 24:31.296 align:middle
So two questions.

24:31.516 --> 24:35.556 align:middle
First, I, I take the sense
of your first question to be,

24:35.556 --> 24:38.446 align:middle
is monetary policy out
of power or out of ammo?

24:38.446 --> 24:40.756 align:middle
And the answer to that would
be "no," I don't think that.

24:40.756 --> 24:45.686 align:middle
I think-I think that we
are strongly committed

24:46.506 --> 24:48.866 align:middle
to using these powerful
tools that we have

24:48.866 --> 24:53.756 align:middle
to support the economy during
this difficult time for as long

24:53.756 --> 24:55.966 align:middle
as needed, and no one should
have any doubt about that.

24:56.656 --> 24:59.926 align:middle
And we do not doubt
the power of the things

24:59.926 --> 25:01.786 align:middle
that we've already
done or the things

25:01.786 --> 25:03.686 align:middle
that we may, may
do in the future.

25:03.686 --> 25:05.806 align:middle
I do think there's
more that can be done.

25:05.806 --> 25:08.706 align:middle
And I also think, if you
look at the stock of assets

25:08.706 --> 25:11.446 align:middle
that we've bought, if you
look at the facilities and,

25:11.446 --> 25:12.646 align:middle
and the way we've been able

25:12.646 --> 25:14.936 align:middle
to keep accommodative
financial-financial conditions

25:14.936 --> 25:17.506 align:middle
accommodative, I think we've
been able to do a lot of things

25:17.506 --> 25:19.906 align:middle
that are providing very strong
support for the economy.

25:19.906 --> 25:22.206 align:middle
And we're going to-we're
going to keep at that.

25:22.936 --> 25:25.696 align:middle
I've said-we've said from
the very beginning, though,

25:25.756 --> 25:29.696 align:middle
that this is particular-that
the particular situation we find

25:29.696 --> 25:33.366 align:middle
ourselves in is one where there
is a sudden loss of income on,

25:33.406 --> 25:36.076 align:middle
on the part of millions and
tens of millions of people.

25:36.766 --> 25:40.746 align:middle
It's not so much a typical
recession where demand weakens,

25:41.166 --> 25:44.066 align:middle
the Fed cuts interest rates,
interest rates stimulate demand,

25:44.066 --> 25:45.196 align:middle
and the economy recovers.

25:45.196 --> 25:47.196 align:middle
It's a sudden shock
where tens of millions

25:47.196 --> 25:48.926 align:middle
of people are out of work.

25:48.926 --> 25:52.786 align:middle
And the fiscal response was,
was frankly, I think, very good

25:52.896 --> 25:55.066 align:middle
and very robust in
the United States.

25:55.706 --> 25:56.636 align:middle
And it's certainly one

25:56.636 --> 25:59.756 align:middle
of the main reasons why
the recovery has been

25:59.786 --> 26:01.256 align:middle
as good as it's been so far.

26:01.636 --> 26:04.376 align:middle
So I do think fiscal policy
is absolutely essential here.

26:04.376 --> 26:06.596 align:middle
You know, stimulating
aggregate demand is one thing,

26:06.596 --> 26:09.476 align:middle
but where there's a part
of the economy that kind

26:09.476 --> 26:12.106 align:middle
of will be resistant to that,
you also need fiscal policy.

26:12.106 --> 26:13.816 align:middle
And, of course, you need
health-care policy too.

26:13.896 --> 26:19.156 align:middle
I didn't see Madame Lagarde's
comments this morning, but I,

26:19.216 --> 26:21.596 align:middle
I took the sense of that
question to be the spread

26:21.596 --> 26:23.766 align:middle
of the disease in Europe, and
what do we think about that?

26:24.076 --> 26:27.966 align:middle
Yes. So, as I mentioned in
my-in my opening remarks,

26:28.426 --> 26:29.346 align:middle
it's a concern.

26:29.346 --> 26:34.096 align:middle
We have a widespread spike
in cases across the country,

26:34.296 --> 26:35.726 align:middle
more in some regions
than others.

26:36.266 --> 26:41.466 align:middle
And even if we don't have-and I
don't expect that we would-sort

26:41.716 --> 26:44.276 align:middle
of government-imposed
restrictions,

26:44.786 --> 26:47.936 align:middle
it does seem likely, though,
that people who have maybe begun

26:47.936 --> 26:51.606 align:middle
to engage in activities
that they had in flying,

26:51.606 --> 26:56.036 align:middle
staying in a hotel, going to
restaurants, going to bars, and,

26:56.036 --> 26:59.386 align:middle
and things like that, that they
may pull back in a situation

26:59.386 --> 27:02.506 align:middle
where suddenly the cases
are everywhere in your city,

27:02.506 --> 27:04.946 align:middle
your state, your community.

27:04.946 --> 27:08.426 align:middle
So I do think that that's a
risk that we have as we go

27:08.426 --> 27:10.816 align:middle
into the fall now and,
and the cases spike.

27:10.816 --> 27:12.556 align:middle
That, that could weigh
on economic activity.

27:12.556 --> 27:13.636 align:middle
One would expect that it would.

27:14.126 --> 27:15.886 align:middle
We thought the same
thing, in fairness,

27:15.886 --> 27:17.396 align:middle
about the wave we
had this summer

27:17.816 --> 27:18.986 align:middle
in the South and the West.

27:19.266 --> 27:20.576 align:middle
And the economy, you know,

27:20.576 --> 27:24.166 align:middle
seemed to move right
through that.

27:24.166 --> 27:26.426 align:middle
This one seems to be
larger and more widespread.

27:26.426 --> 27:27.656 align:middle
In any case, it's a risk,

27:27.656 --> 27:29.636 align:middle
is what I would characterize
it-I would characterize it

27:29.636 --> 27:31.836 align:middle
as a risk, as I did
in my-in my comments.

27:31.836 --> 27:32.536 align:middle
DAVID GURA.

27:32.706 --> 27:33.676 align:middle
Can I ask a quick follow-up,

27:33.676 --> 27:35.846 align:middle
just about the passions
of the Fed Chairman?

27:35.846 --> 27:37.436 align:middle
We've listened to your
rhetoric over the course

27:37.436 --> 27:41.246 align:middle
of this pandemic, and you talked
about what the Fed was doing,

27:41.246 --> 27:42.656 align:middle
the reasons for doing
it at first.

27:42.656 --> 27:43.706 align:middle
You talked about the balance

27:43.706 --> 27:45.466 align:middle
between monetary
and fiscal policy.

27:45.756 --> 27:47.246 align:middle
I think in your recent comments

27:47.246 --> 27:49.086 align:middle
and in Governor Brainard's
comments, certainly,

27:49.086 --> 27:51.896 align:middle
there's been more passion yet
about the need for Congress

27:51.896 --> 27:54.866 align:middle
to do more-I know that you're
limited in what you can say

27:54.866 --> 27:57.456 align:middle
or the degree to which you'd
want to advise Congress,

27:57.456 --> 28:00.336 align:middle
but a simple question: Do you
feel that you're being heard?

28:00.566 --> 28:04.946 align:middle
As you look at the prospects for
this economy and potential need

28:04.946 --> 28:08.036 align:middle
for more fiscal policy, do you
feel like those who are crafting

28:08.036 --> 28:10.476 align:middle
that policy, or could
be, are listening to you

28:10.476 --> 28:12.456 align:middle
and have a firm grasp of
what you're saying about how

28:12.456 --> 28:15.226 align:middle
that might affect or improve
the economy going forward?

28:15.266 --> 28:15.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:15.476 --> 28:18.026 align:middle
So let me-let me say, in
the first instance, our,

28:18.106 --> 28:20.086 align:middle
our main focus is
on doing our job,

28:20.686 --> 28:22.486 align:middle
and that is really what
we're, we're focused

28:22.486 --> 28:24.766 align:middle
on is using the tools
that Congress has given us

28:25.026 --> 28:26.626 align:middle
and the assignment
they've given us.

28:27.026 --> 28:28.996 align:middle
And I think we're,
we're-that is the thing

28:28.996 --> 28:30.336 align:middle
that we think about
night and day.

28:30.936 --> 28:34.026 align:middle
I just know from, from the
experience of the last cycle,

28:34.786 --> 28:36.886 align:middle
it helps to have the
whole government working

28:36.886 --> 28:39.106 align:middle
on these things, and this
one is particularly that way.

28:39.526 --> 28:44.356 align:middle
And I, I don't-I think-you
know, I, I don't want

28:44.356 --> 28:46.136 align:middle
to say whether I feel like
I'm being heard or not.

28:46.136 --> 28:47.806 align:middle
But, sure, I think there
are plenty of people

28:47.806 --> 28:50.246 align:middle
on Capitol Hill who, on
both sides of the aisle,

28:50.246 --> 28:51.816 align:middle
on both sides of the
Hill, who see a need

28:51.816 --> 28:54.326 align:middle
for further fiscal action
and understand perfectly why

28:54.326 --> 28:55.196 align:middle
that might be the case.

28:55.386 --> 28:55.966 align:middle
DAVID GURA.

28:56.206 --> 28:56.516 align:middle
Thank you.

28:56.516 --> 28:56.956 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:59.626 --> 29:00.076 align:middle
Thank you.

29:00.076 --> 29:01.866 align:middle
Edward Lawrence, Fox Business.

29:01.866 --> 29:02.766 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

29:03.166 --> 29:05.736 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking the question.

29:05.976 --> 29:09.556 align:middle
So what would cause the
Federal Reserve to shift more

29:09.556 --> 29:12.536 align:middle
of its asset purchases towards
the long-term securities

29:12.536 --> 29:16.086 align:middle
and Treasuries and change the
amount of spending there also?

29:16.246 --> 29:19.486 align:middle
And as a second point
onto that, you know,

29:19.486 --> 29:22.256 align:middle
would-if there's no
fiscal stimulus package,

29:22.306 --> 29:25.256 align:middle
would that then trigger buying
of more long-term assets

29:25.256 --> 29:26.756 align:middle
or change the asset purchases?

29:26.856 --> 29:27.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:27.916 --> 29:33.216 align:middle
So I don't really have a
specific hypothetical I would-I

29:33.216 --> 29:34.206 align:middle
would put to you.

29:34.456 --> 29:36.426 align:middle
I would just say that
we, we understand

29:36.426 --> 29:38.546 align:middle
that there are a number
of parameters that we have

29:38.546 --> 29:41.036 align:middle
where we can shift the
composition, the duration,

29:41.856 --> 29:45.616 align:middle
you know, the size, the life,
life cycle of the program.

29:45.616 --> 29:49.526 align:middle
All of those things are,
are available to us as ways

29:49.526 --> 29:52.416 align:middle
to deliver addition-you
know, more accommodation

29:52.416 --> 29:53.956 align:middle
if we think that's appropriate.

29:53.956 --> 29:55.256 align:middle
Right now, we, we
like the amount

29:55.256 --> 29:57.866 align:middle
of accommodation the
program is, is delivering.

29:58.386 --> 29:59.606 align:middle
And it will just depend

29:59.606 --> 30:01.216 align:middle
on the-on the facts
and circumstances.

30:01.216 --> 30:03.446 align:middle
We may reach a view at
some point that we need

30:03.446 --> 30:04.486 align:middle
to do more on that front.

30:04.956 --> 30:07.226 align:middle
Today's meeting was
about analyzing the-one

30:07.226 --> 30:10.136 align:middle
of the things it was about was
about analyzing the various ways

30:10.136 --> 30:13.386 align:middle
and having a, you know, good
discussion about how to think

30:13.386 --> 30:15.826 align:middle
about those, those various
parameters, which I,

30:15.826 --> 30:17.426 align:middle
I thought was quite
a useful discussion.

30:17.426 --> 30:18.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:21.776 --> 30:22.216 align:middle
Thank you.

30:22.216 --> 30:23.086 align:middle
Victoria Guida.

30:23.866 --> 30:28.326 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

30:28.486 --> 30:29.146 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

30:29.146 --> 30:30.426 align:middle
Thanks for taking the question.

30:30.426 --> 30:33.426 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
climate change.

30:33.426 --> 30:36.216 align:middle
FHFA Director Mark
Calabria recently said

30:36.216 --> 30:38.046 align:middle
that financial regulators
need to account

30:38.046 --> 30:42.176 align:middle
for financial change-climate
change, I'm sorry.

30:42.656 --> 30:44.006 align:middle
What are you all
thinking of doing

30:44.006 --> 30:46.396 align:middle
on that front beyond what
you're already doing,

30:46.396 --> 30:48.546 align:middle
and do you have any plans
for joining the network

30:48.546 --> 30:49.746 align:middle
for greening the
financial system?

30:50.426 --> 30:50.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:50.636 --> 30:57.566 align:middle
Yes. So I, I do think that
the public, public will expect

30:58.116 --> 31:03.166 align:middle
and has every right to expect
that we-that in our oversight

31:03.166 --> 31:06.446 align:middle
of the financial system, we will
account for all material risks

31:06.446 --> 31:09.556 align:middle
and try to protect the economy
and the public from those risks.

31:09.616 --> 31:11.906 align:middle
Climate change is, is
one of those risks.

31:12.406 --> 31:15.236 align:middle
It's a relatively-the
science and art

31:15.236 --> 31:19.166 align:middle
of incorporating climate
change into our thinking

31:19.166 --> 31:22.446 align:middle
about financial regulation is
relatively new, as you know.

31:22.966 --> 31:27.516 align:middle
And we are, you know, very
actively, in the early stages

31:28.046 --> 31:29.886 align:middle
of this, getting up to speed,

31:29.886 --> 31:32.616 align:middle
working with our central bank
colleagues and other colleagues

31:32.616 --> 31:34.596 align:middle
around the world to try to think

31:34.596 --> 31:36.366 align:middle
about how this can be
part of our framework.

31:36.796 --> 31:38.736 align:middle
And we're watching what
other-what other countries

31:38.736 --> 31:39.046 align:middle
are doing.

31:39.046 --> 31:42.806 align:middle
We're active participants,
as you know, in the CG-no,

31:42.806 --> 31:45.156 align:middle
the greening the
financial system.

31:45.666 --> 31:48.756 align:middle
And, you know, we haven't,

31:48.756 --> 31:50.616 align:middle
we hadn't actually formally
joined, but we're there.

31:50.616 --> 31:52.406 align:middle
We're in the working groups,
and we're doing all of that.

31:52.406 --> 31:53.606 align:middle
So we're very much working with

31:53.606 --> 31:56.216 align:middle
and monitoring the things
other central banks are doing.

31:56.216 --> 31:59.306 align:middle
You hear-you know, there's
lots of research going

31:59.306 --> 32:01.756 align:middle
on at the various Reserve
Banks and here at the Board

32:01.966 --> 32:03.286 align:middle
in trying to understand this.

32:03.716 --> 32:05.506 align:middle
These tend to be
longer-term risks.

32:05.506 --> 32:08.376 align:middle
But, of course, the longer
term does arrive over time.

32:08.846 --> 32:12.846 align:middle
And, you know, we take it as
our obligation to-you know,

32:12.896 --> 32:15.216 align:middle
to understand these risks
and incorporate them

32:15.216 --> 32:17.996 align:middle
into our-into the
way we supervise and,

32:17.996 --> 32:20.276 align:middle
and think about the, the
overall financial system

32:20.276 --> 32:20.926 align:middle
and the economy.

32:20.926 --> 32:21.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:24.586 --> 32:25.066 align:middle
Thank you.

32:25.066 --> 32:26.356 align:middle
Howard Schneider, Reuters.

32:26.446 --> 32:27.396 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

32:30.736 --> 32:32.976 align:middle
Quick follow-up from
Gina's question:

32:32.976 --> 32:35.076 align:middle
Have you gotten a commitment
from Secretary Mnuchin

32:35.076 --> 32:39.226 align:middle
to extend the 13(3) facilities
if you decide it's necessary,

32:39.226 --> 32:42.716 align:middle
and are you concerned what a
lame-duck Trump Administration

32:42.716 --> 32:44.766 align:middle
might do in that regard?

32:44.766 --> 32:48.696 align:middle
And then, more broadly, on
the state of the economy,

32:48.926 --> 32:51.196 align:middle
are you comfortable
now that the tail risk

32:51.296 --> 32:54.356 align:middle
for worst-case outcomes has
been kind of swept aside

32:54.456 --> 32:56.656 align:middle
and minimized at this point?

32:56.656 --> 32:58.356 align:middle
Are we down, in other words,

32:58.356 --> 33:01.996 align:middle
to kind of household-level
problems among a large set

33:01.996 --> 33:06.586 align:middle
of households, perhaps, but
that the financial crisis,

33:06.586 --> 33:07.956 align:middle
double-dip recession sort

33:07.956 --> 33:09.736 align:middle
of scenarios are,
are off the table?

33:09.806 --> 33:10.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:10.456 --> 33:13.256 align:middle
So, on your first question,
we, we really are just turning

33:13.256 --> 33:16.256 align:middle
to this issue now, and we
have not made any decisions.

33:16.256 --> 33:19.096 align:middle
We, we are just getting started
on it, and, and it's a decision

33:19.096 --> 33:20.856 align:middle
that we'll make jointly with
the Treasury Department.

33:20.856 --> 33:23.206 align:middle
And that's, that's really
all I can say today.

33:23.206 --> 33:24.486 align:middle
That's all I have for
you on that today.

33:24.486 --> 33:26.276 align:middle
In terms of the tail
risks, I mean,

33:26.276 --> 33:28.666 align:middle
I think clearly the tail
risks that we were worried

33:28.666 --> 33:30.766 align:middle
about have, have subsided.

33:31.476 --> 33:33.046 align:middle
And, you know, we were worried

33:33.046 --> 33:36.646 align:middle
about very negative
potential outcomes.

33:36.646 --> 33:39.746 align:middle
And that-you know, that's,
that's-what is to be expected

33:39.746 --> 33:43.366 align:middle
of us is, is to think about
how things can go wrong as well

33:43.366 --> 33:44.696 align:middle
as the way things
that can go right.

33:44.696 --> 33:46.986 align:middle
But we, we have to-we
do make policy

33:46.986 --> 33:48.416 align:middle
from a risk-management
standpoint.

33:48.416 --> 33:50.086 align:middle
We don't just look at
the most likely case.

33:50.086 --> 33:53.516 align:middle
We, we ask, "How do you make
policy in light of the risks?"

33:53.516 --> 33:56.906 align:middle
and often it's downside risks
in a situation like this.

33:57.036 --> 34:00.626 align:middle
I would not say that
anybody's feeling comfortable

34:00.626 --> 34:01.316 align:middle
about this, though.

34:01.316 --> 34:07.366 align:middle
You know, we've gotten
through the first five,

34:07.366 --> 34:12.526 align:middle
six months of the-of the
expansion better than expected,

34:12.906 --> 34:16.206 align:middle
but we do see in Europe-look
what's happening in Europe,

34:16.206 --> 34:18.846 align:middle
look what's happening
here-another spike in cases

34:18.846 --> 34:21.756 align:middle
as the cold weather arrives
and people are inside more.

34:22.166 --> 34:25.136 align:middle
So I think we have to be humble
about where we are relative

34:25.136 --> 34:26.016 align:middle
to this-to this disease.

34:26.016 --> 34:27.076 align:middle
It hasn't gone away.

34:27.666 --> 34:31.066 align:middle
Clearly, you know,
therapeutics are advancing,

34:31.066 --> 34:33.126 align:middle
research on vaccines
are advancing,

34:33.126 --> 34:36.016 align:middle
death rates are way down,
hospitalization rates are,

34:36.016 --> 34:38.706 align:middle
are lower now, but-so
we're learning.

34:38.706 --> 34:42.226 align:middle
But, I mean, we, we are
so-we're very far from saying

34:42.226 --> 34:45.756 align:middle
that we think we've got this
and eliminated the tail risks.

34:45.756 --> 34:48.696 align:middle
But I think, clearly,
the tail risks have,

34:49.336 --> 34:51.626 align:middle
have diminished since-at
least our perception

34:51.626 --> 34:53.786 align:middle
of them has diminished
since earlier in the year.

34:53.786 --> 34:54.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:57.296 --> 34:57.866 align:middle
Thank you.

34:57.866 --> 34:58.676 align:middle
Scott Horsley, NPR.

34:58.676 --> 34:59.236 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

35:05.526 --> 35:07.226 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

35:08.276 --> 35:12.216 align:middle
Earlier this summer, I know that
you formed a task force to look

35:12.216 --> 35:14.796 align:middle
at the distribution of
coins around the System.

35:14.796 --> 35:16.666 align:middle
I wonder if you can give us
an update on how that's going.

35:16.666 --> 35:19.456 align:middle
I know there's been some,
some public relations effort.

35:19.456 --> 35:22.276 align:middle
I think October was "Get
the Coins Moving" month.

35:22.276 --> 35:24.666 align:middle
Has that-has that
problem subsided?

35:24.836 --> 35:25.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:25.286 --> 35:28.476 align:middle
So let me say why it's
such an important-you know,

35:28.476 --> 35:30.716 align:middle
in a world where so many
payments are made digitally

35:30.716 --> 35:33.896 align:middle
and all that, coins and
currency are very important

35:34.136 --> 35:38.226 align:middle
for the relatively
low-income people.

35:38.906 --> 35:41.536 align:middle
And so it's a really
incredibly important part

35:41.536 --> 35:44.636 align:middle
of the payment system, and we
do pay a lot of attention to it.

35:45.196 --> 35:48.116 align:middle
And I actually just-I just
caught up this morning

35:48.116 --> 35:51.986 align:middle
with the-with the person who
heads that operation here who,

35:52.326 --> 35:55.066 align:middle
who says that, yes, things have
really gotten significantly

35:55.066 --> 35:56.846 align:middle
better on the coin front.

35:56.846 --> 35:58.836 align:middle
So we worked very hard
to increase the supply

35:58.836 --> 36:01.346 align:middle
of coins and, even more than
that, the distribution of coins

36:01.346 --> 36:03.596 align:middle
around the System
and are happy to say

36:03.746 --> 36:08.616 align:middle
that that situation is well on
the way to normalizing itself.

36:08.616 --> 36:14.966 align:middle
So very pleased to report that.

36:15.486 --> 36:16.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:17.506 --> 36:17.956 align:middle
Thank you.

36:17.956 --> 36:19.346 align:middle
James Politi, FT.

36:19.346 --> 36:21.836 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

36:22.596 --> 36:26.016 align:middle
Thanks very much for the-for
the question, Chair Powell.

36:26.256 --> 36:28.756 align:middle
We've just had a
presidential election here

36:28.756 --> 36:29.596 align:middle
in the United States.

36:30.126 --> 36:32.516 align:middle
We still don't know
who the winner is.

36:32.766 --> 36:36.016 align:middle
Are you concerned that
perhaps tensions or uncertainty

36:36.336 --> 36:37.186 align:middle
over the outcome

36:37.186 --> 36:39.916 align:middle
of the presidential
election could pose a risk

36:40.116 --> 36:42.936 align:middle
to the economic outlook
or trigger market turmoil?

36:43.336 --> 36:46.526 align:middle
And has the Fed had any
discussions, either internally

36:46.656 --> 36:49.106 align:middle
or with the Treasury
Department, about responding

36:49.106 --> 36:50.336 align:middle
to that, should it happen?

36:50.336 --> 36:50.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:50.576 --> 36:55.436 align:middle
Thanks. So I'm very
reluctant, as you will imagine,

36:55.436 --> 36:59.456 align:middle
to comment on the election
directly, indirectly, at all,

36:59.456 --> 37:02.316 align:middle
other than just to say that it's
a good time to take a step back

37:02.316 --> 37:06.166 align:middle
and let the institutions of
our democracy do their jobs.

37:07.146 --> 37:12.146 align:middle
So, at the Fed here, we will, as
always, continue to do our jobs.

37:12.146 --> 37:13.106 align:middle
Every day we'll continue

37:13.106 --> 37:14.846 align:middle
to serve the American
people using our tools

37:14.846 --> 37:18.686 align:middle
to support the economy
during this difficult time.

37:19.346 --> 37:21.976 align:middle
You know, the-you ask
if we had discussions.

37:21.976 --> 37:24.986 align:middle
I would say, you know, the
meeting we just finished,

37:24.986 --> 37:28.826 align:middle
for example, what we do is
we talk about the economy

37:28.886 --> 37:31.406 align:middle
and markets, domestically
and around the world.

37:31.406 --> 37:33.056 align:middle
We hear reports on
how households

37:33.056 --> 37:34.046 align:middle
and businesses are doing.

37:34.046 --> 37:35.436 align:middle
We talk about risks
to the outlook.

37:36.136 --> 37:38.576 align:middle
We talk about what the right
policy response might be.

37:39.356 --> 37:45.666 align:middle
And the election comes up in
some-you know, it comes up now

37:45.666 --> 37:48.426 align:middle
and again, but it is not at all
a central focus of the meeting.

37:48.426 --> 37:49.936 align:middle
Not at all.

37:49.936 --> 37:51.856 align:middle
So I, I'll just leave
it at that.

37:51.856 --> 37:55.526 align:middle
Again, very reluctant to get
into anything more than that.

37:55.876 --> 37:56.786 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

38:01.476 --> 38:01.866 align:middle
Thank you.

38:01.866 --> 38:02.466 align:middle
Anneken, CNN.

38:02.466 --> 38:03.216 align:middle
ANNEKEN TAPPE.

38:03.216 --> 38:06.006 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

38:06.006 --> 38:10.236 align:middle
Chairman Powell, can you
tell us a little bit more

38:10.236 --> 38:12.476 align:middle
about why the minimum loan size

38:12.476 --> 38:15.486 align:middle
under the Main Street facility
was reduced last week and why

38:15.486 --> 38:17.856 align:middle
that was done now
rather than earlier,

38:17.856 --> 38:20.826 align:middle
seeing that small businesses are
still-clearly still-struggling

38:21.026 --> 38:23.726 align:middle
to make the change necessary?

38:24.186 --> 38:24.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:25.486 --> 38:30.626 align:middle
Sure. So, we have had very
little demand below $1 million

38:31.196 --> 38:31.946 align:middle
in loans.

38:32.066 --> 38:36.996 align:middle
And part of that just is that
the fee structure is what it is,

38:36.996 --> 38:39.146 align:middle
and that's the compensation
that the banks get.

38:39.146 --> 38:41.746 align:middle
And there's a certain amount
of work that they have to do

38:41.746 --> 38:45.166 align:middle
to get-to get into the
loan program and do-and

38:45.166 --> 38:47.606 align:middle
to document the thing,
the loans that they make

38:48.096 --> 38:49.436 align:middle
because these are
loans that, you know,

38:49.436 --> 38:50.236 align:middle
go on their balance sheet.

38:50.236 --> 38:51.906 align:middle
They keep 5 percent,
so they actually have

38:51.906 --> 38:52.746 align:middle
to underwrite the credit.

38:52.746 --> 38:53.646 align:middle
And we're relying on them,

38:53.646 --> 38:57.066 align:middle
to some extent, for,
for doing that.

38:57.176 --> 38:58.126 align:middle
And so we were reluctant.

38:58.126 --> 39:00.546 align:middle
We moved the minimum
down to $250,000,

39:00.776 --> 39:02.606 align:middle
and we were reluctant
to go below that.

39:03.266 --> 39:06.836 align:middle
But we heard, over and over
again, that it would be great

39:06.836 --> 39:08.706 align:middle
if we could reduce
it to $100,000.

39:08.706 --> 39:10.516 align:middle
So-and, you know, we've
been saying we'd have

39:10.516 --> 39:11.656 align:middle
to redesign the program.

39:11.656 --> 39:12.716 align:middle
And I, I got the question

39:12.986 --> 39:16.156 align:middle
in a-in an oversight
hearing a month or so ago

39:16.156 --> 39:18.206 align:middle
with the Secretary
and said that.

39:18.206 --> 39:20.596 align:middle
And I went back to the office
and thought, you know, okay,

39:20.596 --> 39:24.626 align:middle
so what would that look like if
we were to redesign the program

39:24.626 --> 39:25.956 align:middle
in a way-what's the
least thing we could do

39:25.956 --> 39:28.266 align:middle
to redesign the program so that
we could move to a lower level?

39:28.266 --> 39:30.836 align:middle
And it-basically, we concluded
we could just change the fee

39:30.836 --> 39:33.956 align:middle
structure to create incentives
for that, so we did that.

39:34.356 --> 39:35.356 align:middle
We try to be responsive.

39:35.356 --> 39:38.606 align:middle
We want, you know, qualifying
businesses to be able to borrow.

39:39.096 --> 39:41.866 align:middle
And we'll see how
much demand will come.

39:42.146 --> 39:43.466 align:middle
You know, with, with
these programs,

39:43.556 --> 39:45.206 align:middle
why did it-why didn't
we do it right away?

39:45.206 --> 39:49.276 align:middle
We tend to-it's-there's more
work than one might-certainly

39:49.276 --> 39:51.646 align:middle
than I imagined in setting
up one of these facilities.

39:52.226 --> 39:54.896 align:middle
And you, you just try to get
it out there and get it working

39:55.146 --> 39:57.036 align:middle
and not try to do
everything before you start it

39:57.036 --> 39:58.426 align:middle
because you'll never
start it if you do that.

39:58.426 --> 40:00.476 align:middle
Then you get it started,
and you make changes.

40:00.476 --> 40:02.266 align:middle
And we've been willing
to make changes.

40:02.266 --> 40:04.936 align:middle
That's the most recent set
of ones that we've done.

40:04.936 --> 40:07.706 align:middle
And, again, we hope-we hope that
it will help some companies,

40:08.336 --> 40:09.936 align:middle
and I guess we'll find out.

40:12.516 --> 40:13.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:22.186 --> 40:22.746 align:middle
Thank you.

40:23.166 --> 40:24.196 align:middle
Heather. HEATHER SCOTT.

40:27.116 --> 40:29.616 align:middle
Heather Scott this time, right?

40:29.616 --> 40:34.546 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell,
for taking the question.

40:34.746 --> 40:38.686 align:middle
You say that you're-that
you're not out of ammo.

40:38.736 --> 40:41.956 align:middle
So I'm wondering, then, what
is the next tool you expect

40:41.956 --> 40:44.146 align:middle
to be able to roll out?

40:44.386 --> 40:47.626 align:middle
Would you consider some sort
of credit program for state

40:47.626 --> 40:51.336 align:middle
and local businesses-state
and local governments

40:51.336 --> 40:53.526 align:middle
that are facing real
budgetary pressures?

40:53.936 --> 40:54.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:55.276 --> 41:01.716 align:middle
So when I say we're not out of
ammo, I'm looking at, you know,

41:01.716 --> 41:03.356 align:middle
a couple of our tools, mainly.

41:03.356 --> 41:05.546 align:middle
As I mentioned, the asset
purchase program-there's a

41:05.546 --> 41:07.676 align:middle
number of dimensions
in which we can adjust

41:07.676 --> 41:09.076 align:middle
that if we deem it
to be appropriate.

41:09.076 --> 41:11.056 align:middle
Right now, we like
the job it's doing.

41:11.706 --> 41:15.856 align:middle
We could, you know, if, if
the facilities are extended,

41:15.856 --> 41:17.626 align:middle
we could certainly
look at new facilities.

41:18.216 --> 41:19.766 align:middle
If-you know, if things
deteriorate,

41:19.766 --> 41:22.726 align:middle
that would be the
case where you'd want

41:22.726 --> 41:27.556 align:middle
to maybe continue the facilities
and, and, and maybe change them

41:27.556 --> 41:28.626 align:middle
and maybe have new ones.

41:28.626 --> 41:29.036 align:middle
Who knows?

41:29.036 --> 41:31.386 align:middle
But, certainly, the facilities
have been doing a lot of work

41:31.656 --> 41:33.076 align:middle
and been very successful,
I think,

41:33.076 --> 41:35.896 align:middle
overall in accomplishing
that job.

41:35.896 --> 41:38.636 align:middle
So I do think there, there
are things that we can do.

41:38.636 --> 41:41.286 align:middle
But, remember, we've always
said this will take a whole

41:41.286 --> 41:44.436 align:middle
of government approach,
including health-care policy

41:44.436 --> 41:45.576 align:middle
and fiscal policy too.

41:45.576 --> 41:49.316 align:middle
So it really is, if you-if you
want to get the economy back

41:49.886 --> 41:52.776 align:middle
as quickly as possible to
where we want it to be, then,

41:52.776 --> 41:55.006 align:middle
then really it should be all
of government working together.

41:55.476 --> 42:02.346 align:middle
HEATHER SCOTT.

42:02.346 --> 42:07.896 align:middle
If I could just follow
up on that quickly.

42:07.896 --> 42:08.696 align:middle
I mean, you spoke [inaudible]

42:08.696 --> 42:11.356 align:middle
about the small business
lending program.

42:11.356 --> 42:14.726 align:middle
But that has only reached
400-there's only been 400 loans.

42:14.726 --> 42:16.736 align:middle
When you say the
programs are effective,

42:16.736 --> 42:19.736 align:middle
do you not think there's
more that can be done

42:19.856 --> 42:23.286 align:middle
with another type of program
or another redesign either

42:23.286 --> 42:25.446 align:middle
for small businesses
or for assisting state

42:25.446 --> 42:26.266 align:middle
and local governments?

42:26.266 --> 42:27.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:27.646 --> 42:29.086 align:middle
Yes. There's a bunch
of programs.

42:29.086 --> 42:30.376 align:middle
I would say that the, you know,

42:30.376 --> 42:33.026 align:middle
the corporate-the larger
corporate market-based lending

42:33.026 --> 42:34.486 align:middle
program has been very successful

42:35.256 --> 42:38.606 align:middle
without making a single
loan-I think, unambiguously,

42:38.606 --> 42:39.686 align:middle
has been a big success.

42:39.686 --> 42:41.176 align:middle
I think the success-I
think the state

42:41.176 --> 42:45.246 align:middle
and local government program has
also restored market function

42:45.246 --> 42:49.106 align:middle
so that-whereas, you
know, there were the,

42:49.296 --> 42:54.646 align:middle
the sort of individual investors
who wind up funding loans

42:54.646 --> 42:55.816 align:middle
in the muni market through.

42:56.136 --> 42:59.426 align:middle
through mutual funds, they, they
had withdrawn a lot of money,

42:59.426 --> 43:02.386 align:middle
and, and that-we've had many
weeks of consecutive, I believe,

43:02.386 --> 43:05.316 align:middle
of, of inflows there,
and you're largely back

43:05.316 --> 43:06.656 align:middle
to a normal functioning market.

43:06.656 --> 43:07.946 align:middle
I think that's also
been a success.

43:07.946 --> 43:09.346 align:middle
I think the other-certainly the,

43:09.346 --> 43:12.316 align:middle
the funding market programs
have been a big success.

43:12.316 --> 43:14.666 align:middle
The-Main Street is just-is
just a bigger challenge

43:14.666 --> 43:15.846 align:middle
than all of them.

43:15.846 --> 43:18.756 align:middle
Reaching out to small and
medium-sized businesses

43:19.166 --> 43:21.066 align:middle
through the banking
system, which we had to do,

43:21.066 --> 43:22.446 align:middle
is, is quite challenging.

43:22.756 --> 43:27.896 align:middle
I do think, you know, the
grant programs, the PPP, were,

43:28.436 --> 43:32.176 align:middle
were a great way to
reach smaller companies.

43:32.176 --> 43:35.006 align:middle
You know, what you hear out
there is that demand-you talk

43:35.006 --> 43:36.256 align:middle
to banks, and they'll say demand

43:36.256 --> 43:38.176 align:middle
for loans is very,
very low right now.

43:38.606 --> 43:39.836 align:middle
Companies are not borrowing.

43:39.976 --> 43:41.606 align:middle
And, and the reason
is that, you know,

43:41.606 --> 43:44.056 align:middle
activity's at a relatively
low level.

43:44.056 --> 43:46.376 align:middle
They don't want to
run up their debt.

43:46.376 --> 43:50.976 align:middle
You know, so I-we put, we put
an awful lot into Main Street,

43:50.976 --> 43:54.266 align:middle
and it is very challenging to
reach a lot of those companies.

43:54.266 --> 43:57.236 align:middle
But I-you know, I think we, we
are reaching many of them now,

43:57.236 --> 44:01.906 align:middle
and, and I hope that the new
changes will help us reach more.

44:02.346 --> 44:05.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:06.476 --> 44:08.166 align:middle
Thank you.

44:08.166 --> 44:10.506 align:middle
Last question to
Michael McKee, Bloomberg.

44:11.156 --> 44:11.896 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

44:11.896 --> 44:15.336 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, there's a small
but growing number of people,

44:15.336 --> 44:18.056 align:middle
including some former
Fed economists,

44:18.056 --> 44:20.836 align:middle
who say you should find ways
to go beyond your mandate

44:20.836 --> 44:24.996 align:middle
to provide additional support to
the economy, which, in essence,

44:24.996 --> 44:28.676 align:middle
would be fiscal support, since
it hasn't come from Congress.

44:28.766 --> 44:31.026 align:middle
One of the suggestions
is buying state

44:31.026 --> 44:32.566 align:middle
and municipal securities
directly.

44:32.566 --> 44:34.566 align:middle
Another is perhaps
following the BOE

44:34.566 --> 44:37.416 align:middle
in increasing asset purchases

44:37.416 --> 44:41.006 align:middle
to support additional
fiscal programs

44:41.006 --> 44:42.736 align:middle
or spending by the Treasury.

44:42.736 --> 44:46.466 align:middle
Is there any circumstance under
which you would consider those

44:46.546 --> 44:47.936 align:middle
or those would be justified?

44:48.186 --> 44:50.456 align:middle
How closely are you willing
to work with Treasury?

44:51.786 --> 44:52.176 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:52.176 --> 44:56.686 align:middle
You know, so I'm going to
take your question literally.

44:56.686 --> 45:00.346 align:middle
And so if the idea is
money-financed fiscal policy,

45:00.346 --> 45:03.396 align:middle
that's not something
that we would consider.

45:03.746 --> 45:08.396 align:middle
So that-what I mean by that
is, is really, you know, the,

45:08.526 --> 45:12.196 align:middle
the central bank is really
funding fiscal activities

45:12.196 --> 45:13.666 align:middle
of the government
fairly directly.

45:13.666 --> 45:14.726 align:middle
No, that's not something we do.

45:14.726 --> 45:16.446 align:middle
We have different jobs.

45:16.716 --> 45:20.076 align:middle
That separation between those
jobs is absolutely critical

45:20.576 --> 45:21.826 align:middle
in our system of government.

45:22.286 --> 45:25.766 align:middle
The-you know, the job of
taxation and spending goes

45:25.766 --> 45:28.556 align:middle
to people who have stood for
election and been elected,

45:28.776 --> 45:29.946 align:middle
and that's the way it should be.

45:29.946 --> 45:32.006 align:middle
There-they have to be
responsible to the electorate.

45:32.006 --> 45:35.366 align:middle
We have a specific, you
know, job to do with,

45:35.366 --> 45:39.186 align:middle
with a specific set of tools
to support maximum employment,

45:39.186 --> 45:40.826 align:middle
stable prices, financial
stability,

45:40.826 --> 45:41.726 align:middle
help the payment system,

45:41.726 --> 45:43.926 align:middle
supervise banks-all the
things we're assigned to do.

45:44.556 --> 45:46.346 align:middle
But we, we, we're
not going to get

45:46.346 --> 45:49.506 align:middle
into financing the
government and, and-by the way,

45:49.506 --> 45:52.356 align:middle
when we buy government bonds,
we don't-it doesn't actually,

45:52.356 --> 45:55.166 align:middle
as you probably know,
doesn't change the amount

45:55.166 --> 45:56.456 align:middle
of government debt outstanding.

45:56.836 --> 46:00.386 align:middle
We issue a reserve to, to
purchase a Treasury security,

46:00.736 --> 46:03.736 align:middle
and that's just another form-on
the consolidated balance sheet

46:03.736 --> 46:07.006 align:middle
of the federal government,
that's just another obligation.

46:07.006 --> 46:09.656 align:middle
You change the-you
change the, you know,

46:09.656 --> 46:12.346 align:middle
the nature of the obligation,
but not the total quantity

46:12.346 --> 46:13.656 align:middle
of debt that the government has

46:13.656 --> 46:15.426 align:middle
when you do these asset
purchase programs.

46:15.426 --> 46:17.506 align:middle
So I don't know exactly
what you're referring to.

46:17.506 --> 46:20.806 align:middle
But, you know, to-again, to
take your question literally

46:20.806 --> 46:23.806 align:middle
about financing fiscal activity,
it's really not something

46:24.456 --> 46:27.306 align:middle
that we think the central
bank should do and,

46:27.306 --> 46:28.866 align:middle
and not something
we're looking at.

46:29.556 --> 46:30.216 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

46:30.216 --> 46:33.236 align:middle
If I could follow up, there is
a suggestion that you buy state

46:33.236 --> 46:36.236 align:middle
and municipal securities
directly in the same way

46:36.236 --> 46:39.776 align:middle
that you buy mortgage-backed
securities, providing, then,

46:39.776 --> 46:43.276 align:middle
cash to municipalities that are
cash-strapped at this point.

46:43.276 --> 46:43.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:44.266 --> 46:47.136 align:middle
Well, we're doing that with the
Municipal Liquidity Facility

46:47.136 --> 46:51.836 align:middle
now, so we're, we're buying, you
know, with, with our own funds

46:51.836 --> 46:54.206 align:middle
and with the CARES Act funds
that, that we've gotten

46:54.206 --> 46:55.486 align:middle
through Congress
and the Treasury.

46:55.486 --> 46:59.306 align:middle
We're buying municipal
securities of up to three years.

46:59.866 --> 47:02.596 align:middle
So we're doing that now as
part of an emergency facility

47:02.596 --> 47:04.856 align:middle
under section 13(3), you know,

47:04.856 --> 47:06.936 align:middle
which has to meet the
emergency requirements

47:07.106 --> 47:08.536 align:middle
of exigent circumstances

47:08.536 --> 47:10.666 align:middle
and which is all clearly
laid out in the law.

47:11.046 --> 47:13.166 align:middle
So we're, we're actually
doing that now.

47:13.656 --> 47:20.436 align:middle
But I, I see-what that is, from
our standpoint, is a rare thing

47:20.436 --> 47:23.986 align:middle
that we do under section 13(3)
when regular intermediation

47:23.986 --> 47:26.646 align:middle
in the capital markets or in the
banking system has broken down.

47:26.976 --> 47:28.166 align:middle
It's no longer working.

47:28.686 --> 47:31.996 align:middle
So we step in under 13(3),
and we provide liquidity.

47:32.366 --> 47:35.136 align:middle
What turns out to happen
is, we announce a program,

47:35.136 --> 47:36.326 align:middle
and the market starts working.

47:36.646 --> 47:38.476 align:middle
So we only actually
have to do a backstop.

47:38.476 --> 47:40.006 align:middle
The amount of financing
that's happened

47:40.006 --> 47:44.006 align:middle
in the municipal markets
this year is much higher

47:44.006 --> 47:46.546 align:middle
than it was the prior year,
and, and we didn't do it.

47:46.546 --> 47:50.496 align:middle
We did-we've done a very
small number of, of loans.

47:50.496 --> 47:51.806 align:middle
But just the fact
that we're there

47:51.806 --> 47:55.976 align:middle
as a backstop seems to-seems
to get the private parties

47:55.976 --> 47:58.896 align:middle
to get back together and, and
get the market working again.

47:58.896 --> 48:01.396 align:middle
So that's what 13(3) is for.

48:02.216 --> 48:04.206 align:middle
We're not-that shouldn't
be a permanent thing

48:04.206 --> 48:07.666 align:middle
where we're just another
federal financing agency that's

48:07.666 --> 48:11.276 align:middle
available to direct
credit to-you know,

48:11.716 --> 48:14.646 align:middle
to very worthy borrowers.

48:14.886 --> 48:17.056 align:middle
That would be more along
the lines of a GSE.

48:17.406 --> 48:18.186 align:middle
That's not what we do.

48:18.186 --> 48:20.156 align:middle
We're there to provide
emergency liquidity

48:20.156 --> 48:24.726 align:middle
when intermediation has broken
down and then to pull back

48:24.726 --> 48:27.216 align:middle
on that when-you know,
when-as the markets normalize,

48:27.216 --> 48:29.966 align:middle
we pull back and we
put those tools away,

48:29.966 --> 48:31.796 align:middle
as we did after the
Global Financial Crisis

48:31.796 --> 48:33.656 align:middle
and as we will here
when the time comes.

48:33.686 --> 48:34.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:36.806 --> 48:39.576 align:middle
Thank you very much.

48:39.736 --> 48:40.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:40.876 --> 48:41.136 align:middle
Thank you.

